\(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots on N,S-doped graphene synthesized via interlayer confinement for enhanced lithium-/sodium-ion storage
a School of Mechanical and Electrical Engineering, North University of China, Taiyuan 030051, China  b State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, China  c Shanxi North Xingan Chemical Industry Corporation Ltd., Taiyuan 030003, China  d CAS Key Laboratory of Molecular Nanostructure and Nanotechnology, Institute of Chemistry Chinese Academy of Sciences (CAS), Beijing 100190, China  e School of Environment and Safety Engineering, North University of China, Taiyuan 030051, China  f Institute for Superconducting and Electronic Materials, School of Mechanical, Materials, Mechatronics and Biomedical Engineering, University of Wollongong, Wollongong 2522, Australia
G R A P H I C A L A B S T R A C T
A \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots/N,S-rGO composite is prepared via interlayer confinement, and delivers decent electrochemical performances for LIBs/SIBs, due to the improved charge transfer kinetics, capacitive-controlled contribution behavior, and structural stability.
## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Article history:  Received 16 January 2022  Revised 22 March 2022  Accepted 27 March 2022  Available online 30 March 2022
Keywords:  Interlayer confinement  Host/guest precursor  \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots  Carbon-based composite nanomaterials  Lithium- and sodium-ion batteries
Rational design of high-capacity nanosized composites as anode nanomaterials is crucial to boosting electrochemical performances towards large-scale application for lithium- and sodium-ion batteries (LIBs and SIBs). The small sizes and homogeneous dimensional size distributions are achieved typically by either the surface confinement on the underlying supports, or the encapsulation confinement within the precursors (such as metal-organic frameworks). Herein, we report the ultrasmall \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots on reduced graphene oxide \((\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S-rGO})\) synthesized via interlayer confinement as anode nanomaterials for LIBs and SIBs. The composite is synthesized by pyrolyzing a host/guest precursor of sodium dodecyl sulfate ion/ \([\mathrm{NiEDTA}]^{2-}\) anions co-intercalated MgAl-layered double hydroxide LDH host, without additional sulfur source. The host/guest-derived interlayer nanoconfinement enables the composite to integrate the advantageous features: low-content active \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots \((1.0\ \mathrm{wt}\%)\) with a mean size of 3. \(8\pm0.5\ \mathrm{nm}\), high-content N,S-rGO \((89.0\ \mathrm{wt}\%)\), as well as a large specific surface area and mesopore size distribution. The composite used as anode nanomaterial exhibits reversible capacities of \(801.2\ \mathrm{mAh}\)
\(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) after 100 cycles at \(100\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for LIBs, and \(207.7\,\mathrm{mAh}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 200 cycles at \(0.1\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for SIBs, which are greatly higher than those of the pristine N,S-rGO without \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots. The enhancement is experimentally supported by the low charge transfer resistance, high capacitive-controlled contribution, and good structural stability. Our guest/host-based interlayer nanoconfinement can promise an effective synthesis strategy for designing various nanosized anodes for electrochemical energy storage.
