## 1. Introduction
Rechargeable lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have been prevailing in the commercial market of both portable electronic devices and electric vehicles. The ever-increasing price and the restriction of limited Li resources have triggered a series of "beyond-LIBs" research to spring up all over the battery field [1–6], most of which are still in the conceptual stage [5,7–9]. Sodium-ion batteries (SIBs), with the similar chemistry to that of LIBs, have been stimulated to be a potential supplement and even an alternative to LIBs in the field of large-scale electric energy storage [2,3,10], owing to the low cost, available resource, and the relatively lower working voltage than that of LIBs. The electrochemical performances of \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}/\mathrm{Na}^{+}\) storage are dependent on anode materials, which are necessarily able to store sufficient ions and exhibit robust structural stability during the charge/discharge cycles [11]. Among those anode materials [12–17], the traditional graphite anode which is well suitable for LIBs does not suit SIBs, owing to the intrinsically low specific capacity, and the sluggish \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\) ions transfer kinetics caused by the large ionic radius of \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\) (1.02 Å). Alternatively, metal sulfides (such as \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\)) have been investigated as conversion-type anode nanomaterials for SIBs, due to the high theoretical specific capacity \((873\,\mathrm{mAh}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\), electrochemical activity, and good electrical conductivity [18]. The conversion-type of materials are, however, limited by the huge volume variation, electrode material degeneration, and sluggish \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\) transfer kinetics. One effective improvement strategy, by decreasing dimensional sizes to the nanoscale, is used to alleviate the large dimensional strains free of fragmentation and thereby offer excellent electrochemical performances [19,20]. Various \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanostructured anode materials were designed and prepared with high electrochemical performances. \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanoparticles embedded in carbon matrices \((\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{C})\) were fabricated using a one-step gas-phase reaction, with nanoparticles ranging from \(20\,\mathrm{nm}\) to \(60\,\mathrm{nm}\), and exhibited a high reversible capacity of \(670\,\mathrm{mAh}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 160 cycles at \(0.1\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for LIBs [21]. \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanoparticles anchored on open carbon nanohel-mets \((\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{CNH}_{3})\) were prepared using \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) template by combining hydrothermal synthesis and pyrolysis. The \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{CNH}_{3}\) composite had a particle size of \(8\pm2\,\mathrm{nm}\) and a \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) content of \(83.8\,\mathrm{wt\%}\), and delivered a cycling performance of \(490\,\mathrm{mAh}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 3000 cycles at \(5\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for LIBs [22]. Durian-like \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) on reduced graphene \((\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{rGO})\) nanocomposites were constructed using a \([\mathrm{NiEDTA}]^{2-}\)-assisted hydrothermal method. The \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{rGO}\) composite possessed a mean particle size of \(200\,\mathrm{nm}\) and a high
\(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) content of \(85.48\,\mathrm{wt\%}\), and exhibited a reversible capacity of \(986\,\mathrm{mAh}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 200 cycles at \(0.2\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for LIBs [23]. \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{P}\)-doped carbon spheres yolk/shell were obtained with a \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) amount of \(88.7\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) and spherical diameter of \(1.72\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\), by using a Ni-metal organic frameworks (MOF) template, and provided a capacity of \(766.8\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 400 cycles at \(0.5\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for lithium storage [24]. \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{reduced}\) graphene oxide \((\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{rGO})\) nanocomposites were synthesized using a hydrothermal method, with a wide particle distribution ranging between \(20\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(50\,\mathrm{nm}\) and a \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) content of \(64.68\,\mathrm{wt\%}\). The composite anode displayed a reversible capacity of \(575\,\mathrm{mAh}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 100 cycles at \(100\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for SIBs [25]. \(\mathrm{NiS}_{1.23}\mathrm{Se}_{0.77}\) nanosheets embedded in hollow mesoporous carbon spheres were fabricated by combining chemical deposition and anion exchange process, with small particles sizes of \(5{-}10\,\mathrm{nm}\) and high \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) content of \(92.3\,\mathrm{wt\%}\). The composite anode nanomaterial provided a reversible capacity of \(499\,\mathrm{mAh}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(0.1\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 60 cycles for SIBs [26]. All the aforementioned \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\)-involved nanostructured anode materials indeed exhibited high electrochemical performances for LIBs or SIBs. Most of the nanoparticles were prepared typically by either the surface confinement on the underlying supports, or by the encapsulation confinement within the precursors (such as MOFs [27]). The nanoparticles, however, tend to aggregate, which could lead to inhomogeneous dimensional size distribution and rapid decay of electrochemical performances. Therefore, it is of great interest to prepare high-efficiency anode materials with small dimensional sizes and homogeneous size distribution via an effective strategy. Herein, we demonstrate that high reversible capacity and long cycling life for LIBs and SIBs is achieved by decorating low-content \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots \((11.0\,\mathrm{wt\%})\) on N,S-doped rGO. The \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots are derived by pyrolyzing a host/guest precursor of MgAl-layered double hydroxide (MgAl-LDH) /(sodium dodecyl sulfate/ \([\mathrm{NiEDTA}]^{2-}\)) dual anions (Scheme 1), via interlayer confinement. LDHs are well-known as one large family of anionic clay compounds with the unique structure based on brucite-like layers [28]. The unique two-dimensional (2D) layered host structures of LDHs provide amazing nanospace for designing and preparing nanomaterials via an interlayer-confinement strategy, i.e., synthesis process and crystal growth of guest anions are confined into the designated nanospace [29], which are converted to specific nanosized morphologies (such as Ir nanodots, carbon nanorings, and even Pt single atom [30]). The as-prepared \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S-rGO}\) composite integrates advantageous features: (1) low-content \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots \((11.0\,\mathrm{wt\%})\) with a much homogenous size distribution of \(3.8\pm0\).
5 nm, due to the interlayer confinement of the LDH host; (ii) highly conductive and stable N,S-rGO network provides sufficient electronic/ionic conductivity, as well as (iii) the large specific surface area and mesopore size distribution. The integrated \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S-rGO}\) indeed exhibits a higher specific capacity than that of its counterpart of N,S-rGO, demonstrating the effectiveness of introducing \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\). The improved charge transfer kinetics, high capacitive-controlled contribution behavior, and good structural stability serve as the advantages for the as-designed \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S-rGO}\).
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials and chemicals
Graphene oxide (GO) was obtained by using the Hummer's method. All the chemicals used, including \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), Al \((\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}[\mathrm{NiEDTA}]\), NaOH, and sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS), were provided by Aladdin Industrial Inc. All chemicals were used by analytically pure, and no further purification was performed unless required. The decarbonized water was used for the synthesis, typically with a conductance of less than \(10^{-6}\;\mathrm{S\;cm}^{-1}\).
2.2. Preparation of \(D S^{-}//N i E D T A)^{2-}{-}M g A l{-}L D H/G O\) precursor
The precursor was prepared using a coprecipitation method. A mixture, consisting of \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (3 mmol), \(\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), (1 mmol), and GO (8.3 mmol), was dispersed into decarbonized water (100 mL) under strong stirring for 30 min to generate the solution A, followed by transferring rapidly into a three-necked flask and stirred strongly in a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere. The other mixture, composed of SDS (0.5 mmol) and \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}[\mathrm{NiEDTA}]\) (0.75 mmol) with a molar ratio of 1: 1.5, was dispersed in decarbonized water (90 mL) under strong stirring for 30 min to yield the solution B, followed by dropping into the solution A using NaOH (1.5 M) to adjust the solution until \(\mathrm{pH}\,=\,10\). The mixed \(\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{B}\) solution was transferred rapidly into a Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave, which was then preserved at the temperature of \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\). The precursor was collected by rinsing alternatively with decarbonized water and alcohol several times until \(\mathrm{pH}\approx7\), followed by drying in a vacuum at \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{h}\).
## 2.3. Preparation of the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S-rGO}\) product
The precursor was pyrolyzed in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}\) (5: 95 V/V) at a temperature-ramping rate of \(5\;\mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{min}^{-1}\), and kept at \(500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\,\textrm{h}\). The \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S-rGO}\) product was obtained via an acidic treatment (HCl, 2 M) at \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(6\,\mathrm{h}\) to selectively remove the formed \(\mathrm{MgO}\) and \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}0_{3}\) that were both derived from the MgAl-LDH.
For comparison, the pristine N,S-rGO was prepared under the same experimental conditions, without \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}[\mathrm{NiEDTA}]\) for the co-intercalation.
## 2.4. Characterization
The powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of all the samples were collected by using a Shimadzu X-ray diffractometer equipped with a filtered radiation (Cu Kα, \(\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~}\)). The morphological visualization for all the samples was carried out using a JEOL transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEM-2100) and Zeiss scanning electron microscope (SEM, Supra 55). The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption/desorption isotherms of all the samples were recorded to calculate the specific surface area and pore size distribution, by using an American Mac automatic adsorption instrument (ASAP-2020). The element type and chemical valence were probed for the samples by
using X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy Analyzer (XPS, Axis Supra) that was operated at a vacuum degree of \(2\,\times\,10^{-9}\) Pa.
XPS examination of the post-cycled \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S-rGO}\) anode nanomaterials for SIBs after 100 cycles at \(1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The half cell was disassembled in an argon-filled glove box, followed by sealing into a closed container filled with argon, and quickly transferring into the XPS chamber for testing. The quantitative element analysis of the sample was conducted by using inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES, Shimadzu) with a wavelength ranging between 160 and \(850~\mathrm{nm}\). The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, 209 F3) was carried out to determine mass contents in air at a temperature-heating rate of \(10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) from room temperature to \(990\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Renishaw Raman spectrometer (RM2000) was operated to measure the graphitical degree of the carbon part in the sample, with a scanning range from \(200~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) to \(40000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and an excitation light source wavelength of \(532~\mathrm{nm}\).
## 2.5. Electrochemical testing
Electrochemical testing was carried out using the CR2032 coin-type cells that were assembled within a commercially available Ar-filled glove box under the conditions of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), \(\mathrm{O}_{2}<0.1\) ppm. To prepare the anode, a mixture that consists of the composite material (70 mg)/polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF, 10 mg)/Super P (20 mg) was manually ground and then coated uniformly onto the surface of the pre-clean copper foil. For assembling the coin-type cells, Whatman glass fibers were used as the separator, lithium and sodium foils were used as the counter electrodes for LIBs and SIBs, respectively. The electrolytes used were 1 M \(\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}\) that was mixed within a mixture of ethylene carbonate/dimethyl carbonate/diethyl carbonate at a weight ratio of 1: 1: 1 for LIBs, and 1 M \(\mathrm{NaClO}_{4}\) within in ethylene carbonate/propylene carbonate at a weight ratio of 1: 1 (5 wt% fluoroethylene carbonate) for SIBs. In addition, the cyclic voltammetry (CV) and electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) were measured on an Autolab electrochemical workstation (PGSTAT 302 N). The CV measurement was performed at a scan rate of \(0.1\,\mathrm{\mV\s^{-1}}\) and within a voltage range between 0.01 and 3 V. The EIS data were recorded within the frequency range between 0.1 Hz and 100 kHz under an open circuit potential. The galvanostatic discharge/charge profiles were collected on commercial battery testers (LAND CT2001A) within the test voltage range between 0.01 and 3 V.
## 3. Results and discussion
