The host/guest precursor was subjected to the calcination in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}\) at \(500\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\ \mathrm{h}\), followed by a selective acidic treatment to remove the inactive \(\mathrm{MgO}\) and \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) (instead of \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\)) formed during the calcination. The obtained product exhibited a strong broad XRD hump visible between \(20^{\circ}\) and \(30^{\circ}\) (Fig. 1a), which is attributed to the carbon species from both the reduced GO (rGO) and the carbonaceous part converted from the \(\mathrm{DS}^{-}/\mathrm{EDTA}\) dual anions during the calcination. In addition, several weak XRD reflection peaks appeared (Fig. 1a), which are indexed to the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) phase (PDF No. 80-0377) [35]. Compared with the synthesis of sulfides reported previously [36,37], the aforementioned results strongly demonstrate the eco-efficient synthesis of \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\), which was obtained by virtue of the Ni part of \([\mathrm{NiEDTA}]^{2-}\) and the S source in \(\mathrm{DS}^{-}\), without inducing other traditional S sources (such as S powder). Furthermore, the SEM image shows the presence of the folded rGO (Fig. 1b), and the TEM image further reveals that \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots, appearing in dark contrast, were homogenously distributed on the underlying rGO (Fig. 1c). The \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots were measured to be approximately \(3.8\pm0.5\) nm by Gaussian fitting the dimensional size distributions (inset of Fig. 1c). The homogeneous size distribution is greatly attributed to the interlayer nanoconfinement between the interlayer spaces of the MgAl-LDH host that actually prevents the aggregation of the formed \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots during the calcination. Such a type of interlayer nanoconfinement was
used recently to prepare Ir nanodots [31], onion-like carbon nanorings [38], as well as two-dimensional ultrathin graphene [39] and N,S co-doped porous carbon [40]. Furthermore, the HRTEM image shows that the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodot was highly crystalline, with a distinct lattice spacing of \(0.322\ \mathrm{nm}\), which corresponds to the (111) plane of \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) (Fig. 1d). In addition, the SEM/EDX mapping images confirm the co-existence of the C, N, S, and Ni elements (Fig. S3). The Ni/S atomic ratio was quantitatively determined to be 0.65: 1.39, which is closely approximate to the theoretic value (1: 2) of the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) phase. For comparison, the N,S-rGO counterpart, without \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots, was prepared under the same experimental protocols using \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}[\mathrm{EDTA}]\). The N,S-rGO counterpart exhibited only the broad XRD reflection peak of amorphous carbon between \(20^{\circ}\) and \(30^{\circ}\) (Fig. S4a) and the co-existing amorphous/graphitic TEM characteristic (Fig. S4b).
The graphitization degree of the carbonaceous part in the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}\mathrm{-rGO}\) composite was further estimated by using the Raman spectroscopy. The composite exhibited the characteristic D and G bands at 1349 and \(1592\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), respectively (Fig. 2a), which are respectively associated to the defective and graphitic carbon species. The intensity ratio of \(\mathrm{D/G}\) bands \((\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{D}}/\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{G}})\) was calculated to be 0.86, which is slightly smaller than that of the N,S-rGO counterpart (0.9) (Fig. S4c) and indicates a relatively lower degree of graphitization. The lower degree of graphitization is expected to facilitate the specific capacities.
The carbon content of the composite was examined using TG analysis. Fig. 2b shows that three apparent weight losses occurred between \(20\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(900\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The first weight loss occurred by \(4.8\%\) between \(20\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which is caused by the evaporation mostly from the physically adsorbed water. The second weight loss of \(91.4\%\) appeared between \(200\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which is due to the carbonaceous combustion and also to the conversion of \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) into NiO [41,42]. The third weight maintained constant after \(600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\),
which is originated from the residual NiO. Therefore, the exact amounts of the carbonaceous part and \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) were \(89.0\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) and \(11.0\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\), respectively. The N,S-rGO, however, had the TG curve with the complete weight loss by \(100\%\), which is characteristic of the pristine carbonaceous materials (Fig. S4d). Considering that the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots were derived from \(\mathrm{DS^{-}}/[\mathrm{NiEDTA}]^{2-}\) anions, the loading content of \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots can be enhanced by increasing the intercalation content of \(\mathrm{DS^{-}}/[\mathrm{NiEDTA}]^{2-}\) anions.
The textural structure of the composite was also determined by recording \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption-desorption isotherm. Fig. 2c shows that the composite possessed a H3-type hysteresis loop with the characteristic of type IV. The specific surface area was calculated to be \(212\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), with the pore size distribution ranged between 3 and \(10\;\mathrm{nm}\) (inset of Fig. 2c), reflecting a narrow mesoporous distribution. By contrast, the N,S-rGO had a much lower specific surface area of was calculated to be \(59\,\mathrm{\m}^{2}\,\mathrm{\g}^{-1}\), with a similar narrow mesoporous distribution between 3 and \(10\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 2d).
The XPS technique was used to probe the valent state of each specie in the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{-}\mathrm{rGO}\) composite. A full survey spectrum corroborated the co-existence of the elements of C, O, N, S, and Ni (Fig. S5). In the C 1 s spectrum (Fig. 3a), four different peaks were centered at 284.5, 285.0, 286.2, and \(289.4\,\mathrm{eV}\), which are ascribed to \(\mathsf{C{-}C}/\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{C C}\), C–N, C–S, and \(\scriptstyle{C=0}\), respectively [43]. These results reflect the N,S co-doping into the rGO resulting from the \(\mathrm{DS^{-}}/\) [NiEDTA]2−anions [31]. In the N 1 s spectrum (Fig. 3b), three distinct peaks were located at 401.2, 399.6, and \(398.2\ \mathrm{eV}\), which correspond to the graphitic, pyrrolic, and pyridinic nitrogen, respectively; and indeed corroborate the \(N\!\cdot\) -doping into rGO [44]. In the S 2p spectrum (Fig. 3c), two characteristic peaks were centered at 161.7 and \(163.8~\mathrm{eV}\), which are assigned to S \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and S \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\), respectively, thereby suggesting the formation of metal sulfide. Another two peaks appeared at 165.2 and \(168.6\,\mathrm{eV}\), which are well related to the presence of C–S bond [45]. The other peak at \(169.2\ \mathrm{eV}\) is attributed to sulfur oxide \((S O_{x})\), which is caused by the oxidation by either air [46] or the residual sulphate of the
\(\mathrm{DS^{-}}\) anion during the calcination [47]. In the Ni 2p spectrum (Fig. 3d), two pairs of spin-orbit doublets were visible: one doublet at 854.8 and \(872.1~\mathrm{eV}\) that correspond to the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}~2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) species, respectively; the other doublet at 856.4 and \(873.9~\mathrm{eV}\) that are attributed to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}~2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}~2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) species, respectively [48]. Therefore, combination of all the aforementioned results confirmed that the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{-}\mathrm{rGO}\) composite was obtained, and had the advantageous characteristics: high-content carbonaceous part \((89.0\;\mathrm{wt}\%)\), low-content \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots \((11.0\;\mathrm{wt}\%)\) with a homogeneous dimensional size distribution \((3.8\,\pm\,0.5\,\mathrm{\nm})\), as well as the large specific surface area and the narrow mesopore size distribution (between 3 and \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\)), which are expected to boost electrochemical performances [49].
The electrochemical performances were tested by measuring CV curves of the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{-}\mathrm{rGO}\) composite as anode nanomaterial for LIBs. Fig. 4a shows that in the reduction scan, there were two reductive peaks at \(1.55~\mathrm{V}\) and \(0.47\mathrm{~V~}\). The former reduction peak is attributed to the conversion from \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) to metallic Ni phase particles and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathbf{s}\) (i.e., \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) storage process into the electrode), and the latter peak is closely associated with the electrolyte decomposition and the formation of the solid electrolyte interface (SEI). In the anodic CV scan, two apparent oxidation peaks were visible at \(1.22\,\mathrm{~V~}\) and \(1.96~\mathrm{V}\), which correspond to the partially reversible decomposition of the SEI layer, and the re-sulfidation from metallic Ni phase to \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) (i.e., the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) release process from the electrode), respectively [50]. In the subsequent second and third cycles, those two reductive peaks disappeared, but two new weak reductive peaks appeared at \(1.32~\mathrm{V}\) and \(1.58\mathrm{~V~}\); which are well recognized to the two different \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) storage steps in the rearranged active material [51], albeit a slight shift of the re-sulfidation peaks from \(2.01\;\mathrm{V}\) to \(2.04\:\mathrm{V}\). In addition, a good overlap occurred between the second and third cycles, which is suggestive of a reversible cycling stability of \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{-}\mathrm{rGO}\) in the subsequent repeated cycles. However, the CV curve of N,S-rGO shows that only one pair of reductive and oxidation peaks (0.58 and 1.16 V) were clearly visible (Fig. S6a), cor
responding to the formation and the partially reversible decomposition of the SEI layer, respectively, which are characteristic of carbonaceous anode nanomaterials.
The charge/discharge profiles of \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) anodes were then recorded at \(100\,\mathrm{mA\,g^{-1}}\) . Fig. 4b shows that the initial discharge and charge capacities were 1637.1 and \(695.9\,\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\) , respectively, yielding a Coulombic efficiency of \(42.5\%\) . The extremely low Columbic efficiency obtained is well-recognized to be that the open mesopores and large specific surface not only enlarges the electrochemical active surface area, but results in the severe formation of SEI [52]. The low Coulombic efficiency and the relatively high charge capacity could deserve further investigation in lowering specific surface and open pores to boost electrochemical performances [43]. For the charge/discharge profiles of N,S-rGO, no oxidation peak at \(1.96~\mathrm{V}\) was found (Fig. S6b), reflecting the discharge feature of the N,S-rGO without \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots. By contrast, the first discharge and charge capacities of N,S-rGO were 1232.1 and \(493.6\;\mathrm{mAh}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively, which are much lower than those of the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) .
Furthermore, the cycling performance was examined for the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) composite, together with N,S-rGO. The \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) anode nanomaterial exhibited a reversible charge specific capacity of \(801.2\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 100 cycles at \(0.1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\) (Fig. 4c), which greatly outperforms that of the N,S-rGO counterpart (491. \(8\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\) ). Considering that the lifetime of such an unstable 2D material is a long-sought-after question, the further investigation of enhancing the cycling lifetime is desired by lowering specific surface area of composite anode nanomaterials, or by creating carbonaceous micro-/nano-structural spheres.
Furthermore, the rate capability was estimated for the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) and N,S-rGO anode nanomaterials (Fig. 4d). The \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) composite exhibited reversible specific capacities of 790.4, 683.3, 633, 572.1, 512.9, 492.6, 436.9, and \(404.6\;\mathrm{mAh}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at the different current densities ranging from 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.8, 1.0, 1.5, to 2.0 A \(\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) , respectively. The reversible capacity of \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) was able to recover to \(887.1\;\mathrm{mAh}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , as the current density was reset back to \(0.1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\) . By contrast, the N,S-rGO anode nanomaterials exhibited much lower reversible specific capacity after 100 cycles at 0.1 \(\mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) and much more inferior rata capabilities at those corresponding current densities. The above-obtained comparison strongly reveals that the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) anode nanomaterials exhibited greatly enhanced specific capacities and rate capabilities than those of the N,S-rGO counterpart. In particular, the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) anode nanomaterials delivered a long-term cycling performance of \(479.3\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\) after the completion of 300 cycles at a high current density of \(500\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Fig. S7). In addition, further details of the comparison of reversible capacity for LIBs are presented in Table S1. The reversible capacity of our \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) anode nanomaterials with the low-content \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) \((11.0\,\mathrm{wt}\%)\) was comparable to those of the TMS anodes reported early for those TMSs with high contents (Table S1).
To get insight into the high rate performance of \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) , the pseudocapacitive behavior was studied by recording the CV curves at varied scan rates. Fig. 5a shows that as the scan rates were increased from 0.1 to \(1.0\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , similar anodic peaks tended to shift to increasingly high potentials and similar cathodic peaks shift to increasingly low potentials, reflecting the pseudocapacitive behaviors. The charge storage mechanism of the battery is quantitatively determined from the Equation (1): \(i_{\nu}=a{\cdot}\nu^{b}\) , where \(i_{\nu}\) represents the current at a certain voltage, \(\nu\) means scan rates, as well as \(a\) and \(b\) are the empirical parameters [53,54]. The \(b\) value approaches to 0.5, a diffusion-controlled process is dominated; while the \(b\) value is approximate to 1, a capacitive process prevails [53,55]. Fig. 5b shows a fitted linear relationship in the logarithmic form of \(i\) and \(\nu\) at those different redox peak potentials. The slopes of the fitting lines, \(b\) values, were quantitatively determined to be
0.8386 and 0.6587 for the reduction and oxidation peaks, respectively, indicating the coexisting diffusion and capacitance contributions and especially the dominated capacitive process contribute to the charge storage process. Furthermore, the relative contribution of pseudocapacitance control is estimated by the Equation (2): \(i_{\nu}=k_{1}{\cdot}\nu+k_{2}\,\nu^{1/2}\) , where \(k_{1}{\cdot}\nu\) and \(k_{2}\,\nu^{1/2}\) mean the contribution of capacitive- and diffusion-controlled processes, respectively [56]. Fig. 5c shows that at \(2.0\,\mathrm{\mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) , the fraction of capacitive contribution reaches \(63\%\) (as illustrated by the orange region). At the different scan rates, an increase in capacitive-controlled contribution is observed (Fig. 5d), with the increasing scan rates from 0.2 to \(2.0\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\) . All these results demonstrated the rapid kinetics of \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) resulting from the dominant capacitive contribution, which is ascribed to the high specific surface area and highly porous nanostructure [57,58].
The Nyquist plots were measured for both anode nanomaterials using the EIS diagram. Both the anode nanomaterials exhibited a two-frequency characteristic Nyquist plot, which consist of a compressed semicircle within the high-medium frequency range and a straight line within the low frequency range. Remarkably, the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\) N,S-rGO anode exhibited both a much smaller semicircle and a higher slope compared with those of the pristine carbonaceous N,S-rGO anode (Fig. S8). The contrast suggests a low charge transfer resistance and a rapid \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion process for the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-rGO}}\) anode [43], thus underpinning its boosted electrochemical performance.
(Fig. S10d). The above-mentioned results clarify the dominant capacitive contribution to enhance the rapid \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\)kinetics. In addition, the Nyquist plots show that the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}-\mathrm{rGO}\) anode exhibited a smaller semicircle and a higher slope than those of the carbonaceous N,S-rGO (Fig. S11). The remarkable difference means a low charge transfer resistance and a rapid \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\)diffusion process, thereby enhancing the rate capability of the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}-\mathrm{rGO}\) anode.
The structural stability was also visualized for the post-cycled \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}-\mathrm{rGO}\) anode for SIBs after 100 cycles at \(100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The SEM image shows that the graphene nanostructures were clearly visible (Fig. S12a), and the TEM image shows that \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots were dispersed on the underlying rGO (Fig. S12b). The post-cycled nanodots were approximately \(6.5\ \pm\ 0.5\ \mathrm{nm}\) (inset of Fig. S12b), via Gaussian fitting the numbers acquired by manually measuring the dimensional sizes of \(>50\) nanodots from those ex situ TEM images. Although the mean size is larger than that of the pre-cycled nanodots \((3.8\pm0.5\ \mathrm{nm})\), no nanodot agglomeration or significant volume expand was observed for the post-cycled nanodots on the rGO, indicating the good structural stability of the low-content \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) on high-content rGO matrix. Furthermore, the chemical stability was examined using XPS for the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}-\mathrm{rGO}\) anode for SIBs cycled after 100 cycles at \(1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The XPS result of the post-cycled \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}-\mathrm{rGO}\) shows that the Ni 2p spectrum (Fig. S13a) exhibited two main binding energies of 858.9 and \(877.8\ \mathrm{eV}\) that are attributed to the characteristic peak of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\ 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\ 2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\), respectively. The doublet at \(854.1\) and \(871.6\ \mathrm{eV}\) were clearly visible, which correspond to the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}\) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}\ 2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) species, respectively. In the S 2p spectrum (Fig. S13b), two pronounced peaks were observed at 162.1 and \(163.2\ \mathrm{eV}\), which are assigned to S \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and S \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\), respectively, and strongly suggest the formation of metal sulfide. Another peak appeared at \(164.2\ \mathrm{eV}\), which is closely associated with the presence of \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{S}\) bond and demonstrates the S-doping into rGO. The other peak at \(167.6\ \mathrm{eV}\) is attributed to sulfur oxide \((\mathrm{SO}_{x})\), which is caused by either the oxidation of air, or the residual sulphate of the \(\mathrm{DS}^{-}\)anion after the calcination at \(500\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). In the O 1 s spectrum (Fig. S13c), one peak was located at \(532.2\ \mathrm{eV}\), corresponding to the \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}\) bond; whereas the other was visible at \(534.8\ \mathrm{eV}\), which is well ascribed to \(\mathrm{C}=0\). Combination of the as-observed XPS results shows the presence of nickel sulfide and metallic \(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}\), rather than nickel oxide, which strongly demonstrates that the \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots indeed follow the conversion-reaction mechanism [50]: \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}+4\mathrm{Na}^{+}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{Ni}+2\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}\). As a result, the post-cycled \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots exhibit the good chemical stability.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we have demonstrated that the low-content \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots on N,S-doped rGO was successfully prepared via a interlayer nanoconfinement of a host/guest precursor. The \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}/\mathrm{N},\mathrm{S}-\mathrm{rGO}\) composite had the advantageous characteristic: low-content \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots \((11.0\ \mathrm{wt}\%)\) with a homogenous size distribution of \(3.8\pm0.5\ \mathrm{nm}\), highly conductive carbonaceous part \((89.0\ \mathrm{wt}\%)\), as well as the large specific surface area and mesopore size distribution. When served as anode materials, it indeed delivered highly enhanced reversible capacities through the enhanced capacitive behavior, when compared with those of the N,S-rGO electrode without \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) nanodots in terms of cycling stability and rate capability for both LIBs and SIBs. The low charge transfer resistance, high capacitive-controlled contribution, and good structural stability provide a convincing rationale for the enhancement of \(\mathrm{NiS}_{2}\) in rGO. Considering that there is a great flexibility of tuning guest anions intercalated between the interlayer galleries of hosts, the interlayer-confinement approach can easily be extended to prepare numerous anode nanomaterials for LIBs/SIBs.
